<< /A 41 0 R /Alt (Network visualization graphic showing interconnected clusters of terms related to traditional knowledge, environmental management, and conservation. Largest nodes include \215traditional knowledge,\216 \215conservation of natural resources,\216 and \215biodiversity,\216 demonstrating keyword prominence and thematic connections.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Bubble chart displaying the appearance and frequency of environmental terms from 2006 to 2024. Terms like \215climate change,\216 \215ecosystems,\216 and \215sustainability\216 become more frequent after 2015, with larger bubbles representing higher term frequency.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Bubble chart mapping research themes on a two-axis grid: development degree \(density\) versus relevance degree \(centrality\). Themes like \215biodiversity conservation\216 and \215climate change\216 appear as high-density, high-centrality motor themes, while \215ecosystems\216 and \215water conservation\216 are relevant but less developed basic themes. Topics such as \215animals\216 are niche, and \215assessment method\216 and \215alps\216 are low in both metrics, indicating emerging or declining themes.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Concept map graphic showing \215Perception Values\216 at the center, with arrows linking to haunted waters, deities \(water spirits\), sacred water sites \(springs, ponds\), mythological creatures \(mermaids\), rainmaking ceremonies, water spirits \(rain gods\), intergenerational secrecy, and gendered roles; spatial context links sacred sites and perception values.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
